message.content:
{
  "summary": "This bill proposes the creation of the Education Freedom Scholarship Act, a state-funded program administered by the department of education. Starting in the 2025-2026 school year, the program would award up to 20,000 scholarships to eligible students who enroll in an approved private school. Half of these scholarships are reserved for students who meet specific income thresholds (up to 300% of the free or reduced price lunch level) or meet specific existing eligibility criteria; the other half are available to any eligible student regardless of income. Eligible students are Tennessee residents entitled to attend public school, excluding those in home schools or certain church-related schools. 

Scholarships equal the base funding amount defined in the Tennessee Investment in Student Achievement (TISA) formula. Funds must first cover tuition and fees, with remaining balances available for textbooks, approved tutoring, transportation, technology, certain academic programs, approved postsecondary exams, and educational therapy. Recipients in grades 3 through 11 must take an annual standardized achievement test. Private schools that enroll recipients must provide these test results to parents and the office of research and education accountability. The bill specifies that participating private schools remain autonomous and cannot be forced to change their policies or curriculum to accept recipients. Students cannot receive these scholarships if they are already in the Tennessee education savings account pilot program or the individualized education account program, nor do they retain the right to receive an individualized education program (IEP) from their local education agency (LEA). 

For the 2026-2027 school year and beyond, the bill establishes rules for increasing the number of scholarships if demand is high and sets a priority order for awarding scholarships when applications exceed availability. Scholarship accounts close upon graduation, enrollment in a public school, completion of 13 school years, voluntary withdrawal, or disqualification.

Additionally, the bill would provide sole state-funded additional allocations to LEAs that experience disenrollment and a decrease in TISA funding. It also proposes a one-time $2,000 state-funded bonus for K-12 public school teachers employed during the 2024-2025 school year. Finally, the bill amends the distribution of privilege tax collected for the construction and maintenance of public school buildings, requiring the state treasurer to consider economic distress, natural disasters, and other demonstrated needs. Most provisions would take effect upon becoming law, except for the privilege tax changes, which would take effect July 1, 2025."
}
